                <pb facs="0479" n="361" sameAs="frus:frus1928v02/pg361"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">125.643/56: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-10-05T17:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October 5,
                                1928—5 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received October 5—11:40
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>748. Your telegram No. 334, October 4, 3 p.m.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Associated Press report of a deadlock is not correct. Status of the matter
                        is unchanged since Legation’s No. 719, September 20, 6 p.m. We are still
                        awaiting word from Wang both as to his efforts to overcome factional
                        opposition to the arrangements offered by him and as to the progress of
                        repairs of consulate.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>My recommendation is that we should not seek to force the issue one way or
                        the other but await the outcome of Wang’s efforts to arrange the
                        matter.</item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
